From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:54:53 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 195/207] dsv41 server: keep runner-owned bytes through the
 strips beside tool calls

AB3 r7 t02 (tool-full w9-bignum-017 s101): a trace cell landed right after
</think> as "[exact, computed by the engine: 2^66 = 73786976294838206464]\n",
then the model opened the lane's python call. The finish strip that hides the
call markup (reply_text.finishedProse) rstripped the prose, and the lane's
segment merge (tool_parse.parse in compute_segments.note) stripped it again,
so the client's content lost the cell's "\n": the record's span was not in
the text.

Each segment now knows which of its content bytes the engine owns
(runner_owned.zig): the runner records the stream already has give each
write's tokens, Generation.base (set per lane segment) puts them in the
segment, and the tokens' bytes, which must spell the decoded text exactly,
give their offsets in the content. Only spans before the first tool envelope
count. Without token bytes, an exact spelling or a record, nothing changes.

The strips stop at owned bytes and never cut into them (reply_text.stripOwned
and keptEnd):
- finishedProse and cutProse (the stream's last delta, via endProse);
- tool_parse.parseOwned, which also gives the span in the parsed content's
  offsets, in the lane's note() and in openai.zig attachCalls;
- the lane merge carries the span (Reply.owned) into the whole content.

Tests:
- runner_owned_http_test.zig, "compute lane: owned trace lines ...": the t02
  shape through the chat route twice, an owned trace line then the lane's
  python call, then a longer one and a client's call, with one record inside
  the python call. Whole and streamed content keep both lines byte-exact.
- "runner projection: HTTP an owned trace line before a call max_tokens
  cut ...": the same line before a call cut by max_tokens, without the lane
  (after a word: a reply led by "[" streams no call, as before).
- runner_owned.zig unit tests: the span by segment base, field, envelope,
  lossy token bytes and foreign content, and the strips with and without it.

22 negative controls, one per guard, each caught by a named test.
